From: stephan-xyz <noreply@redox-rpi>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] aarch64: ISB after the TTBR write, before the TLB flush

set_table wrote TTBR0/TTBR1 and went straight to the TLB flush. Until a
context synchronization event the core may still use the old table base,
so an out-of-order core (the Cortex-A72 of the Pi 4 and Pi 400) can walk
the old table speculatively after the TLBI and keep those entries. The
next address space then sees pages of the previous one, e.g. code where
its TLS block should be (redox-rpi #332).
---
--- a/rmm/src/arch/aarch64.rs
+++ b/rmm/src/arch/aarch64.rs
@@ -92,6 +92,12 @@
                     asm!("msr ttbr1_el1, {0}", in(reg) address.data());
                 }
             }
+            // Make the new table base take effect before the TLB flush. Without
+            // the ISB the core may still walk the old table after the MSR, and
+            // an out-of-order core (Cortex-A72) can refill the TLB from it after
+            // the TLBI: the next address space then reads the pages of the
+            // previous one through those stale entries.
+            asm!("isb");
             Self::invalidate_all();
         }
     }
